WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.213
Marketing is more than promotion.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:06.814
It connects what people need with a service the library
can actually deliver.

3
00:00:07.014 --> 00:00:14.436
In our fictional example, interviews identify people
seeking predictable quiet periods and others seeking
small-group work.

4
00:00:14.636 --> 00:00:18.749
These are needs-based groups, not assumptions about age
or background.

5
00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:23.434
Some potential users may face barriers or may not know
the service exists.

6
00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:27.836
The chapter's three Cs are customer, convenience, and
communication.

7
00:00:28.036 --> 00:00:29.519
Who needs the service?

8
00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:31.398
How easy is it to use?

9
00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:33.609
How will people learn what it offers?

10
00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:39.673
The six Ps add detail: product, price, place, process,
participants, and promotion.

11
00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:48.833
At Cedar Crossing, consider the service itself, any
charge, where it is available, how booking works, the
people delivering it, and how it is promoted.

12
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:54.253
As an applied extension, also consider users' time and
effort as access costs.

13
00:00:54.453 --> 00:01:00.030
An attractive announcement cannot fix unavailable hours
or a confusing booking process.

14
00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:06.483
Marketing must match capacity, and staff need enough
information and preparation to deliver the promise.

15
00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:11.664
Low use is a reason to investigate, not proof that a
community has no need.

